    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-113517",
      "knowledge_point_id": "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent",
      "problem": "$\\int \\frac{1}{(9-5x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (9-5x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=9-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (9-5x)^{-3}\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{-3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}){u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10{u}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(9-5x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot (-2)(9-5x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(9-5x)^{-3}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =(9-5x)^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(9-5x)}^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
