    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-43308",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e85a2d68-d49f-5bf4-ab62-13ad8c35b0d4",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Expression Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to $(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, use the identity $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ) & =(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we expand the parentheses in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
