  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947936",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65218",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e85a2d68-d49f-5bf4-ab62-13ad8c35b0d4",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Expression Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "$2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x)=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the identity $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ to further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}-\\frac{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =2-2{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =2(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡x=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x$ to rewrite the expression on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x)=2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$",
