    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-23526",
      "knowledge_point_id": "23637774-8d69-500d-8cb0-5e41a0207542",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Rational Expressions Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "$\\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=$",
      "worked_solution": "Since both fractions are over a common denominator, we can add them. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $ to simplify the expression in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$",
