    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-119864",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b05f4be1-95d4-5137-bbaa-71c95862bb59",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Expressions by Factoring an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "Simplify the expression $\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1}=\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =-(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1}=-(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
