      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule",
      "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{{x}^{2}+3x+2}=$",
      "worked_solution": "Note that plugging in $x=-1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{{x}^{2}+3x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}+3x+2]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+\\frac{x}{x+2})}{2x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1+2)+\\frac{-1}{-1+2})}{2(-1)+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-1}{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
