      "knowledge_point_id": "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule",
      "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}=$",
      "worked_solution": "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1]^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
